// Chapter 6, "Pattern Work." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-11):
// 14 CFR 91.113 (right of way, current text incl. Amdt. 91-381, July 24, 2025), 91.123 (ATC clearances and instructions), 91.125 (ATC light
//   signals table), 91.126 (Class G: turns to the left, communications with an operating tower), 91.129 (Class D: communications, VFR radio
//   failure: VFR minimums, visual contact with the tower, clearance to land; approaches circle to the left; (i) takeoff, landing, taxi clearance),
//   read through the eCFR versioner API, Title 14 as of 2026-10-01: https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-10-01/title-14.xml?part=91&section=91.113
//   (same call for 91.123, 91.125, 91.126, 91.129) and https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91
// AIM, current HTML edition:
//   chapter 2 section 1 (VASI and PAPI obstruction clearance, REIL, runway edge lights white with yellow caution zone on instrument runways,
//   threshold green and end red, taxiway edge blue and centerline green, runway guard lights, beacon white and green, daytime beacon in
//   Class D can mean below VFR): https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap2_section_1.html
//   chapter 2 section 3 (runway markings white and taxiway and holding markings yellow, designators from magnetic azimuth, aiming point about
//   1,000 ft, touchdown zone in 500 ft increments, displaced threshold arrows, arrowheads and threshold bar, demarcation bar, chevrons, runway
//   holding position markings with the solid lines on the hold side, not clear of the runway until all parts cross the marking, mandatory
//   signs red with white, location signs black with yellow, closed runway crosses):
//   https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap2_section_3.html
//   chapter 4 section 2, para 4-2-13 (radio failure arriving at a tower: remain outside or above the Class D surface area, acknowledge by rocking
//   the wings by day, blinking landing or nav lights at night; departing: move ailerons or rudder by day):
//   https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap4_section_2.html
//   chapter 4 section 3 (4-3-3 traffic patterns: propeller airplanes 1,000 ft AGL, enter level abeam midpoint, crosswind turn beyond the departure
//   end within 300 ft of pattern altitude, final at least 1/4 mile, 45 degree departure turn, parallel runway cautions; 4-3-13 light gun table and
//   acknowledgment): https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap4_section_3.html
//   chapter 7 section 4 (wake turbulence: heavy, clean, slow; vortices sink several hundred fpm, drift 2 to 3 knots near the ground; light
//   quartering tailwind; landing behind a larger aircraft stay at or above its path and land beyond its touchdown point; departing behind, rotate
//   prior to its rotation point; 2 minutes after a larger aircraft's low approach or touch and go):
//   https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_4.html
// Airplane Flying Handbook, FAA-H-8083-3C: chapter 6 crosswind takeoff (full aileron into the wind at the start of the roll, downwind rudder,
//   side skipping, hold it on slightly longer, crab after liftoff):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/07_afh_ch6.pdf
//   chapter 8 airport traffic patterns (45 degree entry at midfield, downwind 1/2 to 1 mile out, lower aircraft right of way but no cutting in,
//   midair facts, high wing blind spot):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/09_afh_ch8.pdf
//   chapter 9 (stabilized approach criteria, go around if not stabilized by 500 ft AGL in VMC, immediately if unstabilized below 300 ft; go-around
//   power, attitude, configuration, carb heat off, flaps in increments, landing expectancy and pride, ground effect is borrowed performance;
//   intentional slips, upwind wing low in a forward slip, airspeed error in a slip, slips with flaps and fuel limits per POH, slips for
//   inoperative flaps and engine failure; crab and wing-low methods, upwind main wheel first, full aileron into the wind at the stop; 0.2 VSO
//   demonstrated crosswind certification; half the gust factor):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/10_afh_ch9.pdf
// Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, FAA-H-8083-25C, chapter 14 (PAPI: more than 3.5 high, 3.2 slightly high, 3.0 on, 2.8 slightly low,
//   less than 2.5 low; light gun figure; transmitter out, acknowledge by rocking the wings by day and blinking the landing light at night):
//   https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-25c.pdf
// Aviation Weather Handbook, FAA-H-8083-28B: surface wind is magnetic in ATIS and ASOS/AWOS voice broadcasts, true otherwise (METAR):
//   https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf
// Private Pilot Airplane ACS, FAA-S-ACS-6C, PA.III.A, PA.III.B (S5: pattern altitude plus or minus 100 ft, airspeed plus or minus 10 knots),
//   PA.IV.A, IV.B (S10: within 400 ft beyond the point), IV.M (R7: fuel flowage, tail stalls with flaps), IV.N:
//   https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/private_airplane_acs_6.pdf
// KAPA: runways 17L/35R 10,001 x 100 (35R and 17L 350/170 magnetic, 358/178 true, precision markings, 4-light PAPI), 17R/35L 7,001 x 75, 10/28 4,800 x 75
//   (103/283 magnetic, runway 10 threshold displaced 400 ft, 2-light PAPI), variation 8E (2015), beacon white-green, tower 118.9, ground 121.8,
//   ATIS 120.3: https://www.airnav.com/airport/KAPA
// FAA From the Flight Deck, KAPA: hot spots, "Monitor Tower" is not a call or a clearance, expected patterns (left 17L/35L, right 17R/35R),
//   parallels 700 ft apart, don't drift into the parallel's departure path, Class B floor 8,000 over the field: https://www.faa.gov/flight_deck/pilot_info/apa.pdf
// 172N maximum demonstrated crosswind of 15 knots: secondary sources only (NTSB report text quoting the information manual, pilot forums quoting
//   Section 4 of the POH). I could not read a 172N POH, so the chapter presents 15 knots as Biscuit's POH figure, and says it's demonstrated, not a limit.
//   Biscuit's approach speed (65 KIAS used in the drill) is also presented only as Biscuit's book number.
// Crosswind math uses the runway numbers as headings (35R = 350, 28 = 280, 10 = 100, 17 = 170 magnetic) and ATIS winds, which are magnetic.
//   AirNav lists 283 for runway 28; that changes the 28 crosswind by less than a knot here and the text says so.
// All weather, ATIS letters, tail numbers other than N4791E, and people's actions are fictional. Dates: September and October 2026, MDT = UTC minus 6.
// Climbs near KAPA stay below 8,000 MSL (Denver Class B floor). Pattern altitude used: 1,000 ft above the 5,885 ft field, about 6,900 MSL.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 6, part: `Part One: The Share`, title: `Pattern Work`, weeks: `September to October`,
  tasks: [`III.B`,`III.A`,`IV`,`II`,`IX`],
  opening: [
    `Ellie flew on the second Saturday in September, at seven in the morning, in fourteen degrees and an altimeter of thirty point one zero, which was the closest Jordan had ever come to ordering weather off a menu.`,
    `Abby flew it from the left seat. That was Claire's condition, and she'd said it in the parking lot with her keys still in her hand: <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Abby flies. Not you. Not yet."</span> Jordan sat on the right with the worksheet on her knee and Claire's weight written in her own hand, because Claire had stepped on Marisol's scale without being asked and then said, <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Don't read it out loud."</span> Ellie rode in back next to Claire, on a folded hangar blanket, in the lucky cleats, with Danny's cap held on by a hair tie looped through the strap. Biscuit came off 35R before the first taxiway and went up over the reservoir like she'd been waiting all summer to show off. Claire hadn't flown in ten years. Over the reservoir she said, mostly to herself, <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Huh."</span>`,
    `Ellie didn't say anything for the first five minutes. Then she pressed her forehead to the window and said, to the window, <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"She's not out of breath."</span>`,
    `On the ramp afterward Ellie stood on the step and looked into the front of the airplane for a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Which one was Daddy's?"</span> Jordan pointed at the left seat. Ellie nodded like she was filing it. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"So that one's yours now."</span> Then she hopped down and asked Marisol if she could weigh herself again, and Jordan stood there with her hand on the door for a while.`,
    `That was September. The rest of it was the pattern.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="8:02 PM">Next three weeks we live in the pattern. Takeoff, crosswind, downwind, base, final, land, do it again until you hate it, then do it until you don't. Tuesdays 7am, Saturdays 7am. Bring a sandwich, you won't get one.</prop>`,
    `Then, on the last Monday in September, a text from a number she'd saved in April under WALT (CLUB).`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Walt Herrera" time="6:15 AM">Six months gone last week. Monthly logbook check tomorrow, before your lesson. I'll be at the fence Saturday.</prop>`,
    `No question mark. Walt didn't use them much. Jordan was in the station kitchen at the end of a shift, eating a granola bar standing up, and she read it twice. Six months. Thirty one hours in her logbook, every one of them dual, every one signed by Abby in green ink. Six months left to get a certificate she'd need, best case, all of them for. She didn't do that math out loud. Her rig partner asked who it was. She said the guy who wanted to buy her airplane. He looked up from the coffee maker. Her airplane. She'd said it in her own voice, out loud, to see how it sounded.`,
    `It sounded fine. That was the strange part.`,
    `The club chat, meanwhile, had found something to fight about.`,
    `<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="9:40 PM">just noticing biscuit is booked tue and sat 7 to 9 for the next three weeks. by "noticing" i mean i wanted sat. jordan i love you but those are prime pancake hours</prop>`,
    `<prop type="chat" from="Marisol Tran" time="9:52 PM">She's doing pattern work. Six landings an hour. Biscuit's tires are mine for three weeks, Russ. Go eat pancakes in a car like a normal person.</prop>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: the rectangle, and everything around it`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The rectangle`,
        body: [`Whiteboard. I'm drawing a runway, then a box around it, and I'm going to name every side of the box, and you're going to fly that box about two hundred times before you solo. Every airport in the country has this box. Once you can fly it here, you can fly it anywhere, which is the whole reason it exists.`,
          `Takeoff, and you're on the departure leg, straight out along the runway centerline. The AIM says don't turn crosswind until you're beyond the departure end and within 300 feet of pattern altitude. Crosswind leg is the short side, 90 degrees off the runway. Downwind is the long side, flown parallel to the runway in the opposite direction from landing, about half a mile to a mile out, at pattern altitude. You hold that altitude until you're abeam the approach end of the runway. Then power back, start down, and when the runway end is about 45 degrees behind your shoulder, turn base. Base is the other short side. Then final, lined up with the centerline, descending to the numbers. The AIM wants the turn to final done at least a quarter mile out. Upwind is the leg parallel to the runway in the landing direction, and you mostly meet it after a go-around.`,
          `Pattern altitude for propeller airplanes is 1,000 feet above the ground unless the Chart Supplement publishes something else. Ours is 5,885 plus 1,000. Call it 6,900. Look at what's on top of us. The Denver Class B floor over this field is 8,000. Eleven hundred feet of breathing room. You know how you found that out. The ACS wants pattern altitude within 100 feet and your speed within 10 knots, and I want it tighter than that, because 7,000 is the number that becomes 7,400 when you're busy.`,
          `Left turns are the default. In Class G at a field without a tower, 91.126 says make all your turns to the left unless markings or lights say right. In Class D, 91.129 says circle to the left unless ATC tells you otherwise, and ATC tells us otherwise all day. Here the tower expects right traffic on 35R and left on 35L, so the two patterns sit on the outsides of the two runways and don't overlap. Those parallels are only 700 feet apart. The tower people at Centennial asked the FAA to put that in writing for pilots: don't drift into the parallel runway's departure path on climb out, especially on windy days, because there might be a jet behind you on the other runway climbing faster than you've ever climbed in your life.`,
          `Coming in from outside, the standard entry is a 45 degree angle to downwind, aimed at the midpoint of the runway, at pattern altitude, level. The AFH says don't descend into the pattern, because descending entries are a collision setup. Here the tower will usually say something like "make right traffic, report midfield downwind," or give you a straight in, and you do what they said. 91.129(i) says no operating on a runway or taxiway, taking off, or landing at a towered airport without a clearance. 91.123 says once you've got an instruction, you follow it, and if you aren't sure what it meant, you ask right away. "Say again" is free. A runway incursion is not.`],
        terms: [[`Traffic pattern legs`,`Departure (upwind after a go-around), crosswind, downwind, base, final.`],[`Pattern altitude`,`1,000 ft AGL for propeller airplanes unless published otherwise. About 6,900 MSL at KAPA.`],[`45 degree entry`,`The standard way to join downwind: a 45 degree angle aimed at midfield, level at pattern altitude.`],[`91.129(f)`,`In Class D, circle the airport to the left unless ATC directs otherwise.`],[`91.129(i)`,`At a towered airport, no runway or taxiway operation, takeoff, or landing without an ATC clearance.`]],
        exam: `Know the leg names, the 45 degree midfield entry at pattern altitude, 1,000 ft AGL for propeller airplanes, crosswind turn within 300 ft of pattern altitude beyond the departure end, and that pattern turns are left unless marked or assigned otherwise.` },
      { h: `Who gives way`,
        body: [`91.113. Short rule, and the test loves it. First line: when the weather lets you see, you look. IFR or VFR, everybody's job is see and avoid. When the rule gives the other airplane the right of way, you give way, and you don't pass over, under, or ahead of it unless you're well clear.`,
          `In order. An aircraft in distress has the right of way over everybody. Converging, same category, about the same altitude: the one on the other's right has it. Easy check: if they're on your right, they win. Different categories have a pecking order. A balloon beats everything. A glider beats any powered aircraft. An aircraft towing or refueling another one beats every other powered aircraft, airships included. And an airship beats the rest. That's the whole list in the current rule. Think of it as whoever can maneuver least gets the most room.`,
          `Head on, both of you turn right. Overtaking, the one being passed has the right of way, and the one passing goes right to pass well clear. On final approach or landing, you have the right of way over aircraft in flight or on the ground, but you can't use it to force somebody off a runway they're trying to clear. And if two of you are approaching to land, the lower one has the right of way, but the lower one can't use that to cut in front of somebody already on final or to overtake them.`,
          `Here's what that looks like in Biscuit. High wing, so the airplane above you and behind you is invisible. The AFH points out the worst case is a low wing airplane descending on top of a high wing one, each of them blind to the other. Most midairs happen in daylight, in good visibility, near airports, at low altitude, often between two airplanes going the same way, and a lot of them in the traffic pattern, on approach. Basically us, six times an hour. So you lift a wing before you turn, you look before you turn base, and you look at final before you turn onto it. Every time. That's not a style choice.`],
        terms: [[`91.113(c)`,`An aircraft in distress has the right of way over all other traffic.`],[`Converging`,`Same category, about the same altitude: the aircraft on the right has the right of way.`],[`Category order`,`Balloon, then glider, then aircraft towing or refueling, then airship, then all other powered aircraft.`],[`Head on`,`Both aircraft alter course to the right.`],[`Overtaking`,`The aircraft being overtaken has the right of way. The overtaking aircraft alters course to the right and passes well clear.`],[`Landing`,`The lower aircraft has the right of way but may not cut in front of one on final.`]],
        exam: `Expect two aircraft converging and a question about who gives way, the category order, head on and overtaking (both go right), and the landing rule with its limit.` },
      { h: `The air a big airplane leaves behind`,
        body: [`Every wing making lift rolls up two vortices off its tips. Counter rotating, outward, upward, and around, and tight. The AIM says most of the energy is within a few feet of the core. They start at rotation and stop at touchdown, because that's when the wing is making lift. They're strongest behind an airplane that's heavy, clean, and slow. Clean means flaps and gear up, which is why a jet that just rotated and cleaned up is the one to worry about.`,
          `What they do next is what saves you. They sink, several hundred feet a minute, slowing down and weakening as they go, and they slow their descent somewhere below the flight path that made them. Near the ground, within 100 to 200 feet, they spread sideways at 2 or 3 knots. A crosswind of 1 to 5 knots can hold the upwind vortex right in the touchdown zone. A light quartering tailwind can push them forward into it. The AIM prints that one in capital letters, so I will too: the light quartering tailwind requires maximum caution.`,
          `So the rules are mostly about staying above and upwind. Landing behind a bigger airplane on the same runway: stay at or above its approach path, note where it touched down, and land beyond that point. Landing behind a bigger airplane that's departing: note its rotation point and land well before it. Taking off behind a bigger airplane: rotate before its rotation point and climb above its path until you can turn away. After a bigger airplane does a low approach, a missed approach, or a touch and go, give it at least 2 minutes. The vortices can be sitting on the runway.`,
          `Tower will tell you "caution wake turbulence." It's information. The AIM says whether or not they warn you, it's on you. When you accept "follow that Citation," you've accepted the spacing and the wake. Our Citations here land by the aiming point markers, about a thousand feet down. On a 10,000 foot runway, landing past that is free.`],
        terms: [[`Wingtip vortices`,`Counter rotating air trailing from the wingtips whenever the wing makes lift. Strongest from heavy, clean, slow aircraft.`],[`Vortex behavior`,`Sinks several hundred fpm, then drifts 2 to 3 knots sideways near the ground. Moves with the wind.`],[`Landing behind a larger aircraft`,`Stay at or above its path and land beyond its touchdown point.`],[`Departing behind a larger aircraft`,`Rotate before its rotation point and climb above its path.`],[`Two minute rule`,`After a larger aircraft's low approach, missed approach, or touch and go, allow at least 2 minutes.`]],
        exam: `Heavy, clean, slow makes the strongest vortices. They sink and drift with the wind. Light quartering tailwind is the worst case. Land beyond the touchdown point of a larger landing aircraft, before the rotation point of a larger departing one.` },
      { h: `Reading the pavement`,
        body: [`Paint has rules. Runway markings are white. Taxiway markings are yellow, and so is anything that means hold or don't go here, even when it's painted on a runway. Remember that one fact and half the paint makes sense.`,
          `The big number at the end of a runway is its magnetic direction divided by ten and rounded. 35 points about 350 magnetic. Our 35R actually points 350, which is lucky for your crosswind math. L, C, and R tell parallels apart. Before the number, at the threshold, you've got the stripes, and the stripe count tells you how wide the runway is, eight for a hundred feet. Then the touchdown zone bars, every 500 feet. The first group, three bars, sits at 500 feet, before the aiming point, two fat white blocks about a thousand feet down. After that the bars keep going in groups of three, two, and one. Side stripes mark the edges. The centerline is dashes.`,
          `Displaced threshold. Somebody moved the start of the landing part of the runway, for a power line or a hill or neighbors. White arrows down the centerline in the displaced part, white arrowheads across the runway, then a ten foot white bar. That bar is where landing starts. The arrow part is still runway: you can take off from it in either direction, and roll out on it landing from the other end. You just can't touch down on it. Runway 10 here is displaced 400 feet. Land on 28 and you get all 4,800. Land on 10 and you've got 4,400. Yellow chevrons are different. Chevrons mean that pavement isn't for anything, not landing, not takeoff, not taxi. A yellow demarcation bar separates a displaced threshold from a blast pad or a taxiway in front of it. Yellow X's mean closed.`,
          `Hold short lines. Four yellow lines across the taxiway, two solid and two dashed. Solid side is your side. You stop on the solid side with no part of the airplane over the line until you've got a clearance. Coming off the runway, you aren't clear of it until every part of Biscuit, tail included, is past those lines. People stop with the tail hanging over the line all the time and then sit there feeling done. Right next to the line there's a red sign with white numbers. Red with white is a mandatory sign: runway ahead, or a place you can't go. The numbers are laid out like the runway: "17L-35R" means the 17L end is to your left. A black sign with yellow letters tells you where you are. Yellow with black tells you where to go.`,
          `Centennial has a sheet the FAA makes for this airport, with hot spots, places where people keep making the same mistake. Hot spot one: pilots told to taxi to 17L and monitor tower roll onto the runway without a clearance. Monitor means listen. It doesn't mean go and it doesn't mean call. You hold short, switch, and wait to hear your number.`],
        terms: [[`Runway designator`,`The magnetic direction divided by 10, rounded. L, C, R for parallels.`],[`Aiming point`,`Two broad white blocks about 1,000 ft from the threshold.`],[`Displaced threshold`,`Arrows and a white bar. The area before the bar is usable for takeoff and for rollout from the opposite direction, not for touchdown.`],[`Chevrons`,`Yellow. Pavement unusable for landing, takeoff, or taxi.`],[`Runway holding position marking`,`Two solid and two dashed yellow lines. Hold on the solid side. You're clear of the runway only when the whole airplane is past it.`],[`Mandatory instruction sign`,`Red background, white letters. Runway holding position, critical areas, no entry.`]],
        exam: `Expect a figure of a runway with arrows, chevrons, or hold lines, and a question about what the pilot may do there. Know the sign colors: red and white mandatory, black and yellow location, yellow and black direction.` },
      { h: `Lights`,
        body: [`At night or in low visibility, the paint goes away and the lights take over. Runway edge lights are white. On an instrument runway the last 2,000 feet, or the last half if that's shorter, go yellow, a caution zone telling you you're running out. The light fixtures at the very end show red toward you on the runway, the end, and green outward toward arriving airplanes, the threshold. Taxiway edges are blue. Taxiway centerline lights are green. Flashing yellow lights where a taxiway meets a runway are runway guard lights. They mean the same thing the hold line means.`,
          `REIL, runway end identifier lights, are two synchronized strobes on either side of the threshold so you can pick the right runway out of a city full of lights. Runway 10 has them. So does 17R/35L.`,
          `The PAPI is four lights in a row on the side of the runway, usually the left. Two white and two red, you're on the glidepath, normally three degrees. Three white, slightly high. Four white, high. Three red, slightly low. Four red, low, and low is the one you fix right now with power. The AIM says a VASI or PAPI gives you obstruction clearance only within 10 degrees of the centerline and a few miles out, and you don't start down on it until you're lined up with the runway. Our runway 28 PAPI is only two lights. One white and one red is on glidepath.`,
          `The rotating beacon flashes white and green for a civil land airport. If our beacon is on in daylight, that often means the weather in the Class D is below 1,000 and 3, and you need a clearance to take off, land, or fly the pattern. Often. The AIM says don't rely on it. Use the ATIS.`],
        terms: [[`Runway edge lights`,`White. Yellow caution zone on the last 2,000 ft or half the length of instrument runways.`],[`Threshold and end lights`,`Green toward arriving aircraft, red toward an aircraft on the runway.`],[`Taxiway lights`,`Blue edges, green centerline. Runway guard lights flash yellow.`],[`PAPI`,`Two white and two red is on glidepath. More white is high, more red is low.`],[`REIL`,`Synchronized flashing lights at the threshold for runway identification.`],[`Airport beacon`,`White and green for a civil land airport. On in daytime may mean below basic VFR.`]],
        exam: `Know the colors, the PAPI combinations, and REIL's purpose. The test likes "two white, two red" and the yellow caution zone.` },
      { h: `When the radio quits`,
        body: [`Biscuit's com radio is older than me. If it dies in the pattern, the tower can still talk to you with a big light gun, a narrow, intense beam in red, green, or white. You need to know the table cold, because the controller can only tell you yes or no about what it thinks you're about to do.`,
          `In the air: steady green, cleared to land. Flashing green, return for landing, and a steady green will come later. Steady red, give way to other aircraft and keep circling. Flashing red, airport unsafe, don't land. Flashing white means nothing in the air. Alternating red and green, exercise extreme caution, anywhere.`,
          `On the ground: steady green, cleared for takeoff. Flashing green, cleared to taxi. Steady red, stop. Flashing red, taxi clear of the runway in use. Flashing white, return to your starting point on the airport. Alternating red and green, extreme caution.`,
          `Answer them. In the air by day, rock your wings. On the ground by day, move your ailerons or rudder. At night, either way, blink your landing light or your nav lights. If your receiver's dead but you can still transmit, the AIM says stay outside or above the Class D until you know the flow, tell the tower who and where you are and that you want light signals, then join the pattern and watch the tower. If your transmitter's dead, you can still listen, so monitor tower and watch for the light. Squawk 7600 either way. 91.129 says with a dead radio under VFR you can land if the weather's at or above VFR minimums, you can see the tower, and you get a clearance to land. With a dead radio, a steady green is a clearance to land.`,
          `If the radio quits on the ramp, you don't go. You call the tower on the phone, or you fix the radio. Usually the second.`],
        terms: [[`Light gun, in flight`,`Steady green land. Flashing green return. Steady red give way and circle. Flashing red airport unsafe.`],[`Light gun, on ground`,`Steady green takeoff. Flashing green taxi. Steady red stop. Flashing red clear the runway. Flashing white return to start.`],[`Acknowledging`,`In flight by day, rock the wings. On the ground by day, move ailerons or rudder. At night, blink landing or nav lights.`],[`7600`,`The transponder code for lost communications.`],[`91.129(d)`,`VFR radio failure in Class D: land if at or above VFR minimums, in sight of the tower, with a clearance to land.`]],
        exam: `The light gun table shows up on almost every test. The common trap is flashing green, which means taxi on the ground and return for landing in the air.` },
      { h: `Wind on the runway`,
        body: [`Wind almost never lines up with the runway, so you split it in two. The part along the runway is headwind or tailwind. The part across it is crosswind. Crosswind is wind speed times the sine of the angle between the wind and the runway. Headwind is wind speed times the cosine. If the angle is more than 90 degrees, the cosine goes negative, and that negative headwind is a tailwind.`,
          `True or magnetic. This is where good students lose points. Runway numbers are magnetic. The wind on the ATIS and from the tower is magnetic too, because they want you to be able to line it up with the runway. The wind in a written METAR or TAF is true. Here, variation is about 8 degrees east, so magnetic is about true minus 8. A METAR wind of 310 is about 302 magnetic. Use the ATIS wind against the runway number and you're comparing apples to apples. Use the METAR wind against the runway and you're off by eight degrees, which on some days is a knot or two of crosswind.`,
          `In the airplane you won't have a calculator, so use the clock. Angle of 15 degrees, a quarter of the wind is crosswind. Thirty degrees, half. Forty five, three quarters. Sixty or more, all of it. Like the minute hand on a clock: 15 minutes is a quarter of an hour. It runs a little high between 30 and 60 degrees and a hair low under 30. Close enough either way.`,
          `Biscuit's POH lists a maximum demonstrated crosswind of 15 knots. Demonstrated means a test pilot did it, in a new airplane, once, and it was fine. It's not a limitation and it's definitely not a goal. The FAA requires that an airplane be shown controllable in a 90 degree crosswind of at least two tenths of its landing configuration stall speed, and the number they actually showed goes in the book. Your number is on your kneeboard. Crosswind 8. Gusts no more than 10 over. Wind 15. Until I change it.`,
          `If the runway you're assigned is over your card, you don't fly it. You ask for another runway. "Unable" is a whole sentence. The tower will make it work or tell you to wait, and either one's fine.`],
        terms: [[`Crosswind component`,`Wind speed × sin(angle between wind and runway).`],[`Headwind component`,`Wind speed × cos(angle). Negative means tailwind.`],[`Clock rule`,`15° = 1/4, 30° = 1/2, 45° = 3/4, 60° or more = all of the wind as crosswind.`],[`Wind references`,`ATIS, ASOS/AWOS voice, and tower winds are magnetic. METARs and TAFs are true.`],[`Maximum demonstrated crosswind`,`The crosswind shown during certification. Not a limitation. Biscuit's POH: 15 knots.`]],
        exam: `Expect a crosswind component chart or a wind and runway pair. Check whether the wind is true or magnetic before you subtract.` },
      { h: `Crosswind takeoffs and landings`,
        body: [`Takeoff first, because it's easier. Line up, yoke turned all the way into the wind at the start of the roll. That holds the upwind wing down. Rudder to keep straight. The airplane weathervanes into the wind on the ground, so most of the time you're holding downwind rudder. As you speed up, the ailerons start to bite and you take some of it out, but never all of it. If the upwind wing comes up, you get side skipping, a row of little hops sideways, and that's how landing gear gets hurt. Let her stay on the runway a touch longer than normal, then lift off with intent, so she doesn't settle back while drifting. Once you're up, turn into the wind, level the wings, and crab to stay over the extended centerline.`,
          `Landing. Two ways to fight drift. Crab: point the nose into the wind enough that your track lines up with the runway. Easy on final. But you have to kick it straight with rudder right before touchdown, and too early or too late is a side load. Wing low, also called a sideslip: rudder to line the nose up with the runway, aileron into the wind just enough to stop the drift. Now you're flying a little sideways, on purpose, nose straight. The AFH recommends wing low in most cases. I want both. We crab on base and early final, then switch to wing low before the roundout.`,
          `Here's the part people feel in their stomach. As you slow down in the flare, the controls get mushier, so you need more aileron and more rudder to hold the same correction. Keep that upwind wing down the whole way. You'll touch down on the upwind main wheel first, then the other one, then the nose. That looks wrong the first ten times. It's right. After the nose comes down, ease off the rudder so the nosewheel doesn't steer you somewhere. Then keep adding aileron into the wind as you slow, until it's all the way over when you stop. The airplane is never done landing until it's parked.`,
          `Gusts. Add half the gust factor to your approach speed. If it's 14 gusting 20, the gust factor is 6, half is 3, and our 65 becomes 68. The AFH also likes partial flaps in a gusty crosswind, and so do I. Don't add the whole gust, and don't add it twice. Extra speed is extra float, and float in a crosswind is just more time to drift.`],
        terms: [[`Crab method`,`Heading into the wind so the track follows the runway. Remove with rudder just before touchdown.`],[`Wing low (sideslip) method`,`Rudder aligns the nose with the runway; aileron into the wind stops the drift. Touch down upwind main first.`],[`Side skipping`,`Small sideways hops during a crosswind takeoff when the upwind wing is allowed to rise.`],[`Gust factor`,`Gust minus steady wind. Add half of it to approach speed.`]],
        exam: `Crosswind takeoff begins with full aileron into the wind. On landing, the wing low method touches down on the upwind main wheel. Add half the gust factor.` },
      { h: `Going around, and the slip`,
        body: [`Here's the most important thing I'll say this month. A go-around is a normal maneuver. It's not a failure. The AFH lists the two things that make people too late to go around: landing expectancy, which is believing it'll work out because it always has, and pride, which is believing going around means you did it wrong. Both of those kill people in good airplanes on nice days.`,
          `When do you go? Any time it isn't right. The AFH says if you aren't stabilized by 500 feet above the field, go around. If it comes apart below 300 feet, go around right then. Stabilized means on a steady glidepath to your point, on speed within plus 10 and minus 5, configured, trimmed, descending between 500 and 1,000 feet a minute, with only small corrections. Also: traffic on the runway, a bounce, a balloon you can't fix, a gust that puts you sideways, a tower that asks you to. You don't need a reason you can explain. You can explain it on downwind.`,
          `How. Power, attitude, configuration. Full power, smoothly, now, and carb heat off. The nose is going to want to come up hard because you're trimmed for slow and you just added a lot of power, and it'll yaw left, so you push forward and add right rudder. Pitch for a climb attitude. If you're very low, the airplane may touch the runway once. That's fine if you keep it straight. Then flaps up in steps, not all at once, so she doesn't sink back. Don't try to climb out of ground effect too soon, that cushion is borrowed and it gets taken back. When you're climbing, move over to the side of the runway so you can see anybody departing below you, and tell the tower.`,
          `Forward slip. You're high on final, and you want to come down fast without picking up speed. Power to idle. Lower a wing with aileron and hold opposite rudder so the nose swings away from the low wing and your path stays on the centerline. Lots of drag, lots of sink, no extra speed. In a crosswind you lower the upwind wing, because that's what keeps you on track. Pitch controls speed. The airspeed indicator can lie to you in a slip because of where the pitot and static ports are, so fly the attitude and the sound. Before the roundout, take it out smoothly. Let go of the rudder too fast and the nose swings and you gain speed.`,
          `Two cautions. Some POHs limit slips with flaps down, for tail airflow reasons, or long slips with low fuel, because fuel slides to one side of the tank. Read ours and do what it says. And slips are the best tool you've got when the flaps don't work, or the engine doesn't. A slip comes off the instant you don't need it. Flaps don't.`],
        terms: [[`Go-around`,`Power, attitude, configuration. Full power and carb heat off, pitch to climb, flaps up in increments.`],[`Stabilized approach`,`Constant glidepath, speed plus 10, minus 5, configured and trimmed, 500 to 1,000 fpm descent. Not stable by 500 ft AGL in VMC: go around.`],[`Landing expectancy`,`Believing the approach will work out because it usually does. A cause of late go-arounds.`],[`Forward slip`,`Wing down, opposite rudder, nose off the path, to lose altitude without gaining speed. Upwind wing down in a crosswind.`]],
        exam: `First concern in a go-around is power. Carb heat off. Flaps up gradually. A forward slip increases descent rate without increasing airspeed, and it's useful with inoperative flaps or an engine failure.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f6s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`II`, title:`Cord`, q:`The left main tire shows white cord on its inside shoulder. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, September 29, 6:40 in the morning. Walt was already in the hangar when Jordan got there, with Biscuit's logbooks open on Marisol's bench and his reading glasses on his head instead of his face, which meant he was done reading and waiting for something else.`,(G.rushedpreflight?`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Abby told me about the Tuesday in May. The one where you skipped what Marisol touched."</span> He put the glasses on his face. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'm going to watch your walkaround. All of it. Don't mind me."</span> He followed her out to the ramp with his coffee and stood two feet from every station on the checklist like a building inspector, and she felt her ears get hot by the left wing and stay hot.`:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Logbook's fine,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'll walk out with you."</span> He didn't say why. He stood by the tail with his coffee while she went down the checklist, and didn't say anything at all, which was louder.`),`She got to the left main gear and crouched. The tire had the usual flat spot shine from a month of landings. Turning it a little by hand, she saw it: on the inside shoulder, a strip about the size of her thumbnail where the rubber was gone and something white and stringy showed through, like the weave inside a bandage.`,`Walt was looking at the hangar roof.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write up the tire and keep Biscuit on the ground until Marisol sees it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`She writes LEFT MAIN TIRE, CORD SHOWING, INBOARD SHOULDER on the squawk sheet and puts the date and her initials on it. Marisol answers on the second ring, says a word Ellie isn't allowed to say, and shows up in eighteen minutes in sweatpants with a new tire in the back of her Tacoma. Walt holds the jack handle without being asked. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I saw it yesterday,"</span> he says, while Marisol works. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Wanted to know if you would."</span> Jordan doesn't know whether to be mad. She decides she isn't, mostly. They're off at 8:05, an hour late, and Abby says the extra hour is coming out of Jordan's sandwich.`,
          why:`A tire showing cord is worn beyond limits, and the preflight is the pilot's last chance to catch it. Pattern work means lots of landings and crosswind side loads on that tire. Write it up, keep the airplane on the ground, and get a mechanic to fix it.`},
        {t:`Fly the lesson with full stop landings only, and keep them gentle.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Walt shakes his head before she's done saying it. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Your gentle landings, or Abby's? Because I've seen both."</span> He points at the white threads. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Cord is the last thing between that tube and the concrete. You don't plan around it. You fix it."</span> She calls Marisol.`,
          why:`Babying a damaged tire still puts the airplane on it at landing speed, and in a crosswind. A blowout on rollout is a loss of control on the runway. The damage you found decides it: it gets fixed first.`},
        {t:`Ask Walt whether the tire is airworthy enough for pattern work today.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Is it okay?"</span> He takes a sip of coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You're the one who found it. You're the one who'll fly on it. What do you think it is?"</span> She tells him. He nods. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Then call her."</span>`,
          why:`Asking is safe, and Walt would have stopped her. But the pilot in command makes the airworthiness call on the preflight, and here the answer is plain: cord showing means the tire gets replaced before flight.`},
        {t:`Fly the lesson and write the tire up on the squawk sheet after.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`Walt sets his coffee on the horizontal stabilizer, which Marisol would kill him for, and crouches next to her with a knee that pops. He puts his thumb on the white spot. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Holding air right now. After six landings on a gusty day, maybe."</span> He stands up. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Call Marisol. I'll wait."</span>`,
          why:`Holding air isn't the standard. Cord showing means the tread and casing are worn through to the structure. Flying on it risks a blowout on landing, which at 50 knots on a runway is a loss of control. Write it up and get it fixed before flight.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s2`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Monitor`, q:`At a towered field, what does Ground's last instruction allow Jordan to do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The ATIS said wind zero six zero at seven, ten miles, few clouds at one two thousand, altimeter three zero zero four, landing and departing runway three five right. Jordan called ground the way the FAA sheet in the briefing room said Centennial wanted it: who, what type, VFR, which way, and the ATIS letter.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, Centennial Ground, runway three five right, taxi via Alpha."</span>`,`She read it back. Runup at the south end of Alpha, by the 35R threshold. Mags, carb heat, mixture, the door top latch, twice. She told ground she was ready for departure, and ground came back with three words: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, monitor tower."</span> Then she rolled up to the hold short line for 35R, with its two solid yellow lines facing her and the red sign with 35R in white beside it, and switched the radio to tower. A Cirrus was on a mile final. The frequency was busy. Abby had her hands folded.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Call tower, holding short of three five right and ready to go.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She gets three words in before a King Air steps on her, and the controller sorts out the squeal and then says, patient, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Aircraft calling, I've got you, standby."</span> Abby, after: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Monitor means they'll call you. They told you that because they're busy. That's why they're busy."</span>`,
          why:`Calling isn't dangerous. It's just what "monitor" asks you not to do. The KAPA sheet says it plainly: monitor means listen, not call. Ground has already told the tower you're coming.`},
        {t:`Hold short of three five right and listen on tower for her call.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She holds. The Cirrus lands. Ninety seconds later, without her saying a word: <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, runway three five right, cleared for takeoff."</span> She reads it back with the runway in it and checks the compass against the painted 35 as she lines up. Abby nods at the compass. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good. People hear monitor and go. That's the hot spot mistake. The FAA sheet lists it at 17L, and people make it at every runway end."</span>`,
          why:`At KAPA, "monitor tower" means switch frequencies and listen. It's not a clearance to enter the runway and not an instruction to call. 91.129(i) requires a clearance before entering a runway or taking off at a towered airport.`},
        {t:`Taxi onto three five right and line up to wait for takeoff.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Abby's hand is on the brakes before the nosewheel gets to the line, and Biscuit stops with her prop spinning over the solid yellow. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Who cleared you onto the runway?"</span> Nobody had. Out on final, the Cirrus was still coming.`,
          why:`Entering a runway without a clearance is a runway incursion, and the same mistake as KAPA's hot spot one. Monitor tower is not line up and wait and not a takeoff clearance. Hold short until cleared.`},
        {t:`Switch back to ground and ask if she's cleared now that she's there.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:1},
          after:`Ground answers kindly and with a little edge. <span class="said" data-who="man">"One Echo, you're on tower now. Monitor tower, they'll call you."</span> Abby says nothing. She's said it all with her eyebrows.`,
          why:`Asking when unsure is the right instinct, and 91.123 says to ask. But ground already told you what to do. Listening on tower at the hold short line was the whole instruction.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s3`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.B`, title:`Seven hundred feet`, q:`Biscuit is climbing out and drifting off course. How should Jordan respond?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Off 35R she pitched for best rate of climb and watched the airspeed. It was a nice morning, the air cool and thick for Centennial, and Biscuit climbed like she was sorry about August.`,`At four hundred feet Abby said, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Look out your left window."</span>`,`Jordan looked. Runway 35L was right there. Closer than it should have been, even for two runways seven hundred feet apart. Biscuit's nose was pointed straight down the runway heading, 350, but the ground under her was sliding to the left. On 35L, a business jet was rolling.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Crab a few degrees right and track the extended centerline of 35R.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Five degrees right. The runway behind her stops sliding. She glances back and the centerline lines up again under her tail like a seam. Off to the left and below them, the jet goes up through a thousand feet faster than seems decent. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Wind's from the east today, so it pushes you west, onto the other runway's departure path. That jet doesn't know you're drifting. Fly your line."</span>`,
          why:`The wind from 060 pushes a departing airplane west, toward the parallel runway. The AFH crosswind climb uses a wind correction angle to track the extended centerline, and the KAPA tower specifically warns pilots not to drift into the parallel's departure path.`},
        {t:`Hold 350, the runway heading tower expects her to fly after takeoff.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Heading isn't track."</span> She reaches over and turns the yoke a bit right herself. The ground stops sliding. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Hold 350 on a day like this and you're over 35L by the time you turn crosswind, and a Challenger is coming up underneath you."</span>`,
          why:`Holding the runway heading in a crosswind lets the wind carry you sideways. The goal is to stay over the extended centerline, which takes a crab into the wind. At KAPA, drifting west on climb out puts you in the other runway's departure path.`},
        {t:`Turn right crosswind early, away from 35L, and climb there.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Not past the end, not near pattern altitude, and nobody said early right."</span> She points down. They're still over the runway. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Fix the drift. Then turn where you're supposed to."</span>`,
          why:`The AIM has you continue straight out until beyond the departure end and within 300 feet of pattern altitude before turning crosswind. Fix the drift with a crab, then turn at the normal point, or as the tower directs.`},
        {t:`Ask tower whether the jet rolling on 35L has Biscuit in sight.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Tower's busy and says, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"One Echo, maintain runway alignment."</span> Which is a polite way of saying fix it. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Talking's good. Talking while drifting isn't. Crab first."</span>`,
          why:`Talking to the tower isn't wrong, but it doesn't stop the drift. Aviate first: correct for the wind so you stay over your own extended centerline, then communicate.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s4`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.B`, title:`The Cub`, q:`Tower says number two, follow the Cub, and Jordan's gaining on it. What should she do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Fourth lap. Right downwind for 35R at 6,900, ninety knots, Biscuit trimmed and quiet. Tower had a lot of people that morning.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, number two, follow the Cub turning right base. Runway three five right, cleared to land."</span>`,`There was a Cub. Yellow, of course, a little shape well out ahead and below, slow as a bicycle, and getting closer fast.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Slow to approach speed and extend her downwind behind the Cub.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Cub in sight, Niner One Echo."</span> Power back to 1,500, ten degrees of flaps on downwind abeam the numbers, and she keeps going past where she'd normally turn, until the Cub is on final and well ahead and turning base won't put her on top of it. Abby writes a little number on her kneeboard. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"It's going sixty and you were going ninety. Somebody has to change. It's always the one behind."</span>`,
          why:`Following another airplane, you're responsible for spacing behind it. You're overtaking, so the Cub has the right of way under 91.113(f), and the tower gave you a sequence under 91.123. Slow down and extend to fit behind it, and confirm you have it in sight.`},
        {t:`Turn base early and land first. She's the lower airplane.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Abby grabs the yoke and rolls them level. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You're higher, actually. And tower said number two. And the rule says lower doesn't get to cut in front of somebody on final. You'd have just met a Cub in your windshield."</span>`,
          why:`The lower aircraft rule in 91.113(g) specifically forbids using it to cut in front of an aircraft on final. You were also number two by ATC instruction, and 91.123 requires you to comply. Cutting in front of slow traffic on base is a classic midair setup.`},
        {t:`Pass the Cub on the right, the way 91.113 says, then turn base.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The rule says pass on the right, sure. On right downwind, right is toward the runway, into the base turn of everybody you can't see."</span> She taps the throttle. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And tower told you to follow it, which means behind."</span>`,
          why:`Overtaking aircraft do alter course to the right. But here the tower sequenced you behind the Cub, and in a pattern the cleaner answer is to slow down and extend. Passing in a busy pattern adds conflict you don't need.`},
        {t:`Quietly S turn on downwind to open up space behind it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It works, roughly. The airplane behind her on downwind, which she hadn't seen yet, calls tower about the Cessna weaving in front of it. Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Slow first. Then extend. S turns are for when slow isn't enough, and then you tell people."</span>`,
          why:`S turns can make space, but unannounced weaving surprises traffic behind you and you're turning away from where you need to look. Reducing speed and extending downwind are simpler, and the tower should know if you can't make the spacing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s5`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.B`, title:`Caution wake turbulence`, q:`Jordan is number two behind a landing Citation in light wind. How should she fly the approach?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Seventh lap. The ATIS had changed to calm, and the wind on the field was doing that nothing that isn't quite nothing.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, number two following a Citation on a three mile final, runway three five right, cleared to land. Caution wake turbulence."</span>`,`Jordan found it. A white jet, gear down, settling toward the runway ahead of her base turn, sliding down a line she was about to join. She remembered the whiteboard. Below and behind.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly her normal approach to the usual spot and watch for bumps.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Short final, and the left wing just drops. Ten degrees, on its own. Biscuit rocks like a rowboat hit by a wake, which is pretty much what happened. Abby takes it, adds power, and goes around without saying a thing. On the upwind she says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Its touchdown was a thousand feet past where you were aiming. You flew into the stuff it left on the runway."</span>`,
          why:`Vortices sink and can linger in the touchdown zone, especially in light winds. A light crosswind can hold one there. Landing short of a larger aircraft's touchdown point puts you through its wake at low altitude. The AIM says stay at or above its path and land beyond where it touched down.`},
        {t:`Stay above the Citation's path and land past where it touches down.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She stays a little high on the PAPI, three white, one red, on purpose. The Citation's tires smoke at the aiming point blocks. She picks the second set of touchdown bars beyond that and puts Biscuit down there, gently, with more runway ahead than she'll use in a week. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ten thousand feet of runway and a small airplane. That's the cheapest wake avoidance there is."</span>`,
          why:`AIM 7-4-6: landing behind a larger aircraft on the same runway, stay at or above its final approach path, note its touchdown point, and land beyond it. Light winds make it worse, because a light crosswind can hold a vortex in the touchdown zone and still air lets vortices last longer.`},
        {t:`Stay below the Citation's path and land short of where it touches down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Below is where it goes."</span> She draws a little downward arrow on her kneeboard. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Vortices sink. Fly under a jet's path and you're flying where its wake is headed."</span> She has Jordan climb back up to the glidepath.`,
          why:`Wake vortices sink several hundred feet per minute behind the generating airplane, so flying below its path is flying into them. Stay at or above the path and land beyond its touchdown point.`},
        {t:`Ask tower for extra spacing behind the Citation and extend her downwind.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`Tower gives her a 360 on downwind without complaint. It works. Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's fine. Time helps. Today the bigger thing is where you put your wheels, so do both."</span>`,
          why:`More time behind a larger aircraft is always good, and asking for spacing is legitimate. But you still need to fly at or above its path and land beyond its touchdown point. Spacing alone doesn't put you above the wake.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Fourteen gusting twenty`, q:`The wind is 300 at 14 gusting 20, and the ATIS only offers the 35s. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, October 3, 6:55 AM. The METAR on her phone said wind three one zero at one four, gusting two zero. The ATIS, when she called it from the car at the gate, said wind three zero zero at one four gust two zero, runway three five right and three five left in use.`,`Walt was at the fence. Chain link, at the end of the hangar row, with a view of everything. He stood there in a canvas Carhartt jacket with his hands in his pockets like a man waiting for a bus he didn't expect to like.`,(G.ignoredwind?`Jordan looked at the card on her kneeboard and thought about the Thursday in May when she'd flown one lap over it, eleven minutes with both hands clamped on the yoke. She hadn't told Walt about that. She was pretty sure she didn't have to. Abby had the look of someone who'd decided not to bring it up and was going to see if Jordan did.`:`Jordan looked at the card on her kneeboard. STUDENT LIMITS. WIND 15. GUSTS NO MORE THAN 10 OVER. CROSSWIND 8. She'd done the math in the car with the engine off and the heater on.`),`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your call,"</span> Abby said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Show me."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask for runway 28. On 35R the crosswind is about 11, over her card. On 28 it's about 5.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She shows Abby the numbers on her kneeboard. 35R: fifty degrees off, 10.7 crosswind, 15.3 in the gusts, which was over Biscuit's demonstrated number too. 28: twenty degrees off, 4.8, 6.8 in the gusts, 13 knots of headwind. Fourteen steady, six over. She calls ground and asks for 28, and ground says <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, runway two eight approved. Expect a short wait."</span> Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Same airport, same wind, different runway, completely different day."</span> At the fence Walt lifts one hand out of his pocket for a second and puts it back.`,
          why:`The tower picks a runway for most traffic, but you can ask for another one. With the wind at 300 magnetic, runway 28 is only 20 degrees off, so the crosswind is under the card, while 35R is 50 degrees off and over it. "Unable" or "request runway 28" is how you keep your personal minimums intact.`},
        {t:`Take 35R. Fourteen knots is under the card's wind limit, and the gusts are only six over.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby taps the third line on the card, the one that says CROSSWIND 8. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You checked two of three. On 35 it's eleven across and fifteen in the gusts."</span> She keys the mic herself. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ground, Niner One Echo, request runway two eight."</span> Jordan's face is hot.`,
          why:`The card has three limits and the crosswind is the one that matters on 35R today: 14 × sin 50° is about 10.7 knots, over the 8 knot limit, and about 15.3 in the gusts. Meeting two limits out of three is still over the card.`},
        {t:`Cancel. A gusting wind is over a student's head no matter which runway they use today.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Canceling is never wrong."</span> She turns the kneeboard so Jordan can see her own numbers. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"But you're inside every line on that card on 28. This is what the card is for. Ask for the runway."</span>`,
          why:`Canceling is always a legitimate decision. But the card was written for exactly this. On runway 28 the wind, gust spread, and crosswind are all inside the limits, so the better decision is to request the runway that fits.`},
        {t:`Use the METAR wind, 310, against 35R. That's forty degrees off, so about nine knots across.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Which way is north in a METAR?"</span> Jordan stops. True. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Runway numbers are magnetic. The ATIS is magnetic. Use the ATIS. You just moved the wind ten degrees in your favor."</span>`,
          why:`METAR winds are referenced to true north. Runway numbers and ATIS winds are magnetic. With 8 degrees of east variation, the METAR's 310 is about 302 magnetic, so the angle to 35R is about 50 degrees, not 40, and the crosswind is about 10.7 knots, not 9.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s7`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Hands into the wind`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Runway 28 was the kid sister. Shorter, narrower, the 28 painted smaller than the big 35s, and from the hold short line Jordan could see the far end, which on 35R you mostly took on faith. The windsock stood out full and leaned away to her left, and every few seconds a gust shook it like somebody had grabbed the end.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, runway two eight, cleared for takeoff. Wind three zero zero at one four gust two zero."</span>`,`So, wind from the right. Compass checked against the paint on the runway. Abby, conversationally: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your hands. Where are they going?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Yoke full right into the wind, easing it off as she gains speed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Into the wind."</span> Yoke full right, on the stop, which feels like steering a parked car. Throttle in, 2,300 on the tach, which means 2,250. Biscuit tries to swing right toward the wind. Less left rudder than she'd figured. The engine's pulling left anyway. Airspeed alive. Forty, and the yoke starts pushing back, so she lets out a little. Fifty. Fifty five. She holds Biscuit on a beat past where she'd normally rotate and then lifts off with intent, and the second the wheels are free the airplane wants to slide left. Nose right, five degrees, crab. Over at the fence, Walt has a hand out of his pocket with some fingers up. A wave, she figures. For Walt, a parade.`,
          why:`AFH crosswind takeoff: full aileron into the wind at the start of the roll, reduced as the ailerons become effective, with rudder to hold the centerline against weathervaning. Hold it on a little longer, lift off firmly, then crab into the wind.`},
        {t:`Ailerons neutral, wings level, rudder to steer until she's fast.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`About 35 knots, a gust gets under the right wing and Biscuit hops sideways. Twice. Abby turns the yoke right with two fingers and holds it there. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Side skipping. Gear legs hate it. Marisol hates it more."</span>`,
          why:`Neutral ailerons let the crosswind lift the upwind wing, which can start side skipping, little sideways bounces that load the landing gear. Start with full aileron into the wind.`},
        {t:`Yoke turned left to keep the nose from weathervaning right.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Right wing comes up ten degrees, right now, and Jordan's stomach goes with it. Abby's hands are already on the yoke. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Into the wind,"</span> she says, in her pass the salt voice. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Always into it. Your feet do the weathervane."</span>`,
          why:`Turning the yoke away from the wind raises the upwind wing, the exact opposite of what you want. Aileron goes into the wind. Weathervaning is handled with rudder.`},
        {t:`Yoke halfway into the wind, then an early liftoff to beat the drift.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Half helps. Early doesn't. Biscuit leaves the runway a few knots before she really wants to, slides left, and kisses the pavement again, crabwise, for one long second Jordan feels in her teeth. Then she flies. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Full in at the start,"</span> Abby says. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And hold her down longer. People get that one backward every fall."</span>`,
          why:`Some aileron is better than none. But the AFH says to start with full aileron and to hold the airplane on the runway slightly longer in a strong crosswind, then lift off firmly, so it doesn't settle back while drifting.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s8`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`Wing low`, q:`Crabbed right on short final for 28, what should Jordan do for the landing?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tower worked them into the pattern for 28. On final, at about four hundred feet, Biscuit was crabbed a few degrees right, the runway sitting off to the left of the nose like a TV seen from the kitchen. The path ran down the centerline anyway. The two PAPI lights were one white and one red.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay,"</span> Abby said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Time to stop crabbing. Show me."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Left rudder to line up the nose, right aileron to stop the drift.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Left rudder. The nose swings onto the runway, and the runway starts sliding right in the windshield as the wind pushes her left, so she drops the right wing. A little. More. There. Now it's banked and pointed straight, and every instinct she owns says no. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ugly is correct,"</span> Abby says. In the flare it all goes mushy and she has to keep adding, aileron and rudder both, like turning up a drip that isn't keeping up. Right main. Left main. Nose. On the rollout she keeps feeding the yoke right until it's against the stop at the turnoff. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Upwind wheel first. I want that one every time."</span>`,
          why:`Wing low method: rudder aligns the longitudinal axis with the runway, aileron into the wind stops the drift. With a right crosswind that's left rudder and right aileron. Hold it through the flare, touch down on the upwind main, and increase aileron into the wind as you slow.`},
        {t:`Hold the crab down to the flare and kick it straight with rudder.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She kicks it straight a hair early. In that hair Biscuit drifts left, and the tires touch a little sideways and chirp like a sneaker on a gym floor. Abby shrugs. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Crab's fine if your timing's perfect."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yours will be, around year three. Today, wing low."</span>`,
          why:`The crab method is legitimate and taught. But it depends on precise timing at the instant before touchdown, and too early or too late is a side load. The AFH recommends wing low in most cases, and Abby asked for it.`},
        {t:`Level wings, right rudder, then let the wind settle her down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Right rudder takes the nose further right, the wrong way. Wings level, so nothing's stopping the drift. Now Biscuit's aimed at the grass on one side and sliding at the grass on the other. Neat trick. She'd like to not do it again. Abby, mildly: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Go around."</span> So they go around.`,
          why:`With a crosswind from the right, the nose is already crabbed right. Aligning it with the runway takes left rudder, and stopping drift takes right aileron. Touching down while drifting puts damaging side loads on the gear.`},
        {t:`Carry ten extra knots down final to cut the drift time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Abby lets her. Seventy five knots on short final, and Biscuit floats past the blocks, drifting left the whole way. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Half the gust factor. That's three. You added ten."</span> Go around. Again.`,
          why:`A gusty crosswind calls for adding half the gust factor, here about 3 knots. Extra speed beyond that adds float, and float in a crosswind is just more time to drift. Correct the drift with a sideslip.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s9`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`IV`, title:`The balloon`, q:`A gust has lifted Biscuit fifteen feet in the flare, nose high and slowing. What's Jordan's move?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Third lap on 28. Sixty eight knots, centerline, right wing down, and going well, which Jordan decided later was the problem. She started the flare at the thousand foot blocks and a gust off the right came through and lifted Biscuit like a hand under a tray. Ten feet. Fifteen. Nose high, 55 knots and unwinding toward the bottom of the white arc, and the blocks slid by underneath. 4,800 feet of runway, minus the ones already behind her.`,`Abby's hands stayed in her lap. Walt was at the fence.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Hold the nose where it is, let her settle, and use the runway left.`, s:0, flag:`forcedlanding`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She holds it. About eight feet up Biscuit quits flying. All of it at once. The right main hits with a bang Jordan feels in her fillings. They bounce. Abby's hand is already on the throttle, full in, and they're going around whether Jordan likes it or not. Nobody talks on the upwind. Then Abby, flat: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"No airspeed, no ideas, and you kept going because there was runway. That has a name. Landing expectancy. It's in the book because people die of it."</span> On the crosswind turn Jordan sneaks a look at the fence. Hands in pockets.`,
          why:`A balloon high above the runway with decaying airspeed is a go-around. Holding the attitude and hoping lets the airplane stall above the runway and drop. The AFH names landing expectancy and pride as the two reasons pilots delay a go-around.`},
        {t:`Add full power and go around, then bring the flaps up in steps.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Throttle in, smooth, all the way, like Abby drilled into her all September. Carb heat off, knob in. Biscuit's nose wants to climb the wall, trimmed for slow and suddenly full of power, and it takes a real push on the yoke and a lot of right rudder to keep her sensible. Flaps 20 to 10. Then up, as the needle comes around. She slides a little right of the runway so she can see down it. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Tower, Niner One Echo, going around."</span> The woman in the tower doesn't sound surprised. She sounds like she's on her second coffee. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, roger, make right traffic, report downwind."</span> Halfway up the upwind Abby says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Dry, quiet, grudging praise.">"Best thing you've done all week,"</span> to the windshield, not to Jordan. At the fence, Walt has both hands out of his pockets.`,
          why:`AFH go-around: power first, smoothly and fully, carb heat off; then attitude, holding the nose down against the trim and adding right rudder; then configuration, flaps up in increments. Sidestep to see departing traffic and tell the tower.`},
        {t:`Push the nose down to get the wheels back on the runway.`, s:0, flag:`forcedlanding`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Abby's hand lands on the yoke right next to hers and stops it cold, way before the nosewheel gets anywhere near the concrete. Then full power, Abby's, and up. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Ten feet, nosewheel first. That's how a nose strut folds and the prop eats the runway."</span>`,
          why:`Shoving the nose down from a balloon risks hitting nosewheel first, which can start porpoising or collapse the nose gear. If the balloon is more than slight, the AFH says go around.`},
        {t:`Add a little power to cushion it, and land on the runway ahead.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It works, technically. Biscuit floats like she's thinking it over, touches down well past where Jordan wanted, and the rollout ends with more of the far end in the windshield than she'd care to describe. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"A little power for a little balloon, sure,"</span> Abby says. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That was not little. On a short runway that's a fence."</span>`,
          why:`A touch of power can soften a slight balloon. A large balloon with airspeed decaying and the touchdown point behind you is a go-around, especially on a 4,800 foot runway. The AFH says when ballooning is excessive, go around immediately.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s10`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`The breaker`, q:`The flap breaker popped on downwind. How should Jordan handle the approach?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Downwind, abeam the numbers, 1,500 RPM, carb heat on, flap switch to 10. Nothing. The indicator stayed at zero. Out the window the flap sat flush with the wing. On the panel the FLAP breaker stood a quarter inch proud of its neighbors, like a kid with a question.`,`Abby saw it the same second. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Leave the breaker. You just lost flaps. Keep flying."</span>`,`So she flew a no flap pattern without meaning to, and on final she was high. Both PAPI lights on 28 white.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pull the power to idle and slip her down to the glidepath.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Idle. Right wing down, left rudder, and the nose swings off left of the runway while the airplane comes down like an elevator, wind roaring along the right side of the cabin. Airspeed needle all over the place, 60, 70, 65. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Don't chase it. Attitude."</span> Nine hundred a minute down on the VSI. At one white, one red, she rolls the wing up and lets the rudder go slowly, and lands no flap, flat, five knots fast, upwind main first. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's what slips are for. Flaps can quit. Slips can't."</span> That afternoon Marisol finds a corroded connector at the flap motor, says it isn't her fault, and fixes it anyway.`,
          why:`With inoperative flaps, a forward slip increases the descent rate without increasing airspeed. In a crosswind, lower the upwind wing. Pitch controls speed, and the airspeed indicator can read wrong in a slip, so fly attitude. Take the slip out smoothly before the roundout.`},
        {t:`Reset the popped breaker to give the flaps one more try on final.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Abby puts her hand over the breaker row. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"A breaker pops because something drew too much current. Pushing it in on short final, with your eyes on the panel, is two problems."</span> They go around.`,
          why:`Resetting a tripped breaker in flight can make an electrical fault worse, and doing it on final takes your attention off flying. Fly the airplane without flaps and let a mechanic find the cause.`},
        {t:`Lower the nose to the glidepath, and bleed the speed off in the flare.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Seventy, eighty, eighty five. Biscuit floats past the aiming point, past the touchdown bars, past half the runway, and Abby says <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Around,"</span> and they go. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Diving trades height for speed. You wanted to trade height for nothing."</span>`,
          why:`Lowering the nose to lose altitude adds airspeed, and without flaps there's even less drag to scrub it off. The airplane floats a long way. A forward slip loses altitude without gaining speed.`},
        {t:`Go around and fly a wider, lower pattern for a normal no flap final.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`Fine. Safe. A long, flat final from a mile and a half out, a little nose high, a long float. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Totally fine. Now you know the airplane can do it without a do-over. Next lap, slip it."</span>`,
          why:`Going around to set up a better approach is always acceptable, and a no flap pattern is flown wider and flatter. But a forward slip would have fixed this approach from where you were, which is why the ACS teaches it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s11`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Steady green`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, October 6, the last week. Right downwind for 35R, clear and still. She keyed the mic to report downwind and heard nothing. No sidetone. She keyed it again. Then she noticed that the frequency, which had been busy all morning, had been silent for a while.`,`Abby flipped the radio to ground, then to the ATIS. Dead air both places. She pushed her own push to talk and shrugged. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Old radio. Your airplane. What do we do?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Squawk 7600 and watch the tower for a light signal.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`7600 on the transponder. Wide downwind, a mile out, 6,900, watching the tower cab like it owes her money. Base. Then a pin of green light from the cab, steady. Cleared to land. She rocks the wings, which feels like waving at a stranger across a parking lot, and the green stays on. 35R, rollout, off at Alpha seven, all the way past the hold lines. Flashing green: taxi. She wiggles the ailerons, feels dumb, and it works. At Hangar Six, Marisol unplugs the mic jack, squints, and holds up a green beard of corrosion on the tip of a screwdriver. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Danny's coffee,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Ten years of him drinking it over the radio."</span>`,
          why:`With a radio failure in Class D, 91.129(d) allows a VFR landing if weather is at or above VFR minimums, you're in sight of the tower, and you receive a clearance to land, which can be a steady green light. Squawk 7600. In flight by day, acknowledge by rocking the wings. On the ground by day, move the ailerons or rudder.`},
        {t:`Land on 35R. They were cleared into the pattern already.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby shakes her head. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Pattern isn't landing. 91.129. You need a clearance to land, and with no radio that's a steady green."</span> She points at the tower. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Look for it."</span>`,
          why:`At a towered airport you need a landing clearance. With a radio failure that clearance comes as a steady green light from the tower, and 91.129(d) lists it as a condition for landing.`},
        {t:`Leave the Class D and land at an untowered field out east.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Could you? Sure. Plenty of fields out east with no tower and no radio needed."</span> Abby points at the cab. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"But we're in the pattern, it's VFR, that tower is staring at us, and there's a light gun up there for exactly this. 91.129. Use it."</span>`,
          why:`Diverting to a non-towered field without a radio can be legal and safe. But you're already in the pattern at a towered airport with VFR weather and a tower in sight, which is the case 91.129(d) and the light gun signals are built for.`},
        {t:`Keep circling, and land when the tower flashes green.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Flashing green, a minute later. Jordan starts her base turn. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Flashing in the air, come back around. Steady, land. Flashing on the ground, taxi. The test loves that one."</span> Steady green on short final, half a mile out.`,
          why:`In flight, flashing green means return for landing, to be followed by a steady green at the proper time. Steady green is cleared to land. On the ground, flashing green means cleared to taxi, which is the mixup the test is fishing for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s12`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`XII`, title:`Every part of the airplane`, q:`What should Jordan do as she exits the runway at Alpha seven?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, October 10. The 7 AM weather: wind calm, four degrees Celsius, altimeter thirty point one eight. Landing number nine. Sixty five knots over the fence, centerline under the nose, mains on about two hundred feet past the aiming point blocks, one chirp from the stall horn. Jordan knew before the wheels touched. Good, not perfect. At the fence Walt's hand went up, and she was too busy to count.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, turn right at Alpha seven, contact ground."</span>`,`Alpha seven came up on the right. She slowed to a walk, turned off, and rolled toward the hold short lines. Dashed side toward her this time. Solid side beyond.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Stop as soon as she's off the runway pavement and call ground.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Biscuit stops with her tail over the dashed lines, which means her tail is still in the runway safety area. The Bonanza on a half mile final gets sent around. Tower, to the Bonanza: <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Traffic exiting at Alpha seven."</span> Nobody says whose. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Off the runway isn't clear of the runway."</span>`,
          why:`AIM 2-3-5: an aircraft exiting a runway is not clear until all parts of the aircraft have crossed the runway holding position marking. Stopping short of it leaves you inside the runway safety area.`},
        {t:`Stop once her tail is past the hold short lines, and call ground.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She lets Biscuit roll until she can see the yellow lines go under the right wing strut, then gives it another length for the tail, then stops. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Ground, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, clear of three five right at Alpha seven, taxi to Hangar Six."</span> Ground gives her the taxi. Abby doesn't say anything. She's writing in her own notebook, and she's smiling at it, which Jordan has never seen her do.`,
          why:`You're clear of the runway when every part of the aircraft has crossed the holding position marking, then you stop and contact ground as instructed. Leaving, you cross the dashed lines first, because they're on the runway side, and the solid lines last. They don't hold you on the way out.`},
        {t:`Stop with the nosewheel on the solid lines and call ground.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Solid side is where you hold going in. Coming out, the solid lines are behind you once you're clear."</span> She points over her shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Right now your tail is still on the runway's side. Keep rolling."</span>`,
          why:`The solid lines are on the side where you hold before entering a runway. Leaving a runway, you cross the dashed lines first and the solid lines last, and you aren't clear until the whole airplane is past them.`},
        {t:`Keep rolling toward Hangar Six once she's past the hold short lines.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She's clear of the runway, which is good. Then she's on Alpha, which isn't hers yet, and ground says, <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna One Echo, hold position."</span> The FAA sheet warns about exactly that.`,
          why:`Getting clear of the runway is right. But at KAPA you don't proceed on taxiways without a ground clearance, and the tower told you to contact ground. Clear the hold lines, stop, and call.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f6s6`, title:`Which runway`,
     setup:[`Saturday morning, Jordan's kneeboard. KAPA's runways, using the runway numbers as headings: 35R and 35L about 350 magnetic, 17L and 17R about 170, 28 about 280, 10 about 100.`,`METAR: <code>METAR KAPA 031253Z 31014G20KT 10SM FEW120 08/M04 A3012</code>. That wind is true. Variation is 8 degrees east.`,`ATIS: wind 300 at 14, gusting 20. That wind is magnetic.`,`Crosswind = wind × sin(angle). Headwind = wind × cos(angle). Negative headwind is a tailwind. Use the ATIS wind against the runway numbers.`],
     fields:[{label:`METAR wind direction converted to magnetic (degrees)`,answer:302,tol:0},{label:`Angle between the ATIS wind and runway 35R (degrees)`,answer:50,tol:0},{label:`Crosswind on 35R, steady (knots)`,answer:10.72,tol:0.05},{label:`Headwind on 35R, steady (knots)`,answer:9.0,tol:0.05},{label:`Crosswind on 35R in the gusts (knots)`,answer:15.32,tol:0.05},{label:`Crosswind on 28, steady (knots)`,answer:4.79,tol:0.05},{label:`Crosswind on 28 in the gusts (knots)`,answer:6.84,tol:0.05},{label:`Headwind on 28, steady (knots)`,answer:13.16,tol:0.05},{label:`Tailwind on runway 10, steady (knots)`,answer:13.16,tol:0.05}],
     solution:[`True to magnetic: with east variation, subtract. 310 minus 8 = 302 magnetic. The ATIS rounds that to 300.`,`35R: 350 minus 300 = 50 degrees. sin 50° = 0.766, cos 50° = 0.643.`,`Crosswind 35R: 14 × 0.766 = 10.72 knots, from the left. Headwind: 14 × 0.643 = 9.00 knots. In the gusts: 20 × 0.766 = 15.32 knots across. That's over Abby's card (8) and over Biscuit's demonstrated 15.`,`28: 300 minus 280 = 20 degrees. sin 20° = 0.342, cos 20° = 0.940.`,`Crosswind 28: 14 × 0.342 = 4.79 knots, from the right. Gusts: 20 × 0.342 = 6.84 knots. Headwind: 14 × 0.940 = 13.16 knots. All inside the card.`,`Runway 10: 300 minus 100 = 200 degrees, which is 160 degrees the other way. cos 160° = minus 0.940, so the headwind is 14 × minus 0.940 = minus 13.16. That's a 13 knot tailwind. Never.`,`Check the error trap: if you'd used the METAR's 310 against 35R, the angle is 40 degrees and the crosswind looks like 14 × 0.643 = 9.0 knots, 1.7 knots better than the truth. And AirNav lists 28 at 283 magnetic. With 283, the 28 crosswind is 14 × sin 17° = 4.09, less than a knot different. The runway number is close enough for a go or no go.`]},
    {after:`f6s9`, title:`Numbers you can do on downwind`,
     setup:[`No calculator in the airplane. Abby wants rules of thumb you can do in your head, then checked against the real math.`,`Wind 300 at 14 gusting 20. Runway 35R. Biscuit's approach speed from her POH, as Abby flies it in gusts with partial flaps: 65 KIAS. Field elevation 5,885 feet.`,`Clock rule: 15° = 1/4 of the wind across, 30° = 1/2, 45° = 3/4, 60° or more = all of it. For 50 degrees, use 50 minutes on a clock, which is 5/6 of an hour.`],
     fields:[{label:`Clock rule crosswind on 35R (knots)`,answer:11.67,tol:0.1},{label:`How much the clock rule overestimates it versus the sine answer (knots)`,answer:0.95,tol:0.05},{label:`Gust factor (knots)`,answer:6,tol:0},{label:`Speed to add for gusts (knots)`,answer:3,tol:0},{label:`Approach speed in the gusts (KIAS)`,answer:68,tol:0},{label:`Pattern altitude, 1,000 feet above the field (feet MSL)`,answer:6885,tol:0},{label:`Room between pattern altitude and the Class B floor (feet)`,answer:1115,tol:0}],
     solution:[`50 minutes on the clock is 50/60 = 5/6. 14 × 5/6 = 11.67 knots. The exact answer was 14 × sin 50° = 10.72. The clock rule is 0.95 knots high. Good. An estimate should err toward more crosswind, not less.`,`Gust factor = gust minus steady = 20 minus 14 = 6 knots. The AFH says add half of it: 3 knots.`,`Approach speed: 65 + 3 = 68 KIAS. Don't add the whole gust, and don't add it on top of some other cushion you've already flown in.`,`Pattern altitude: 5,885 + 1,000 = 6,885. Abby flies 6,900.`,`The Class B floor over KAPA is 8,000 MSL. 8,000 minus 6,885 = 1,115 feet. That's the whole margin for a busy go-around climb. Level off on purpose.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f6s11`, title:`Paint and light`, intro:`That night Jordan made Abby's flash cards into a quiz for herself on the back of a run report. For each one, pick what it means.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`In flight, a steady green light from the tower.`, choices:[`Cleared to land`,`Return for landing`,`Cleared to taxi`], answer:0, why:`Steady green in flight is cleared to land (91.125). On the ground it's cleared for takeoff.`},
      {prompt:`In flight, a flashing green light from the tower.`, choices:[`Cleared to land`,`Return for landing, steady green to follow`,`Airport unsafe, do not land`], answer:1, why:`Flashing green in flight means return for landing, followed by a steady green at the proper time. On the ground it means cleared to taxi.`},
      {prompt:`In flight, a steady red light from the tower.`, choices:[`Airport unsafe, do not land`,`Stop`,`Give way to other aircraft and continue circling`], answer:2, why:`Steady red in flight: give way and keep circling. Flashing red in flight: airport unsafe, don't land.`},
      {prompt:`On the ground, a flashing red light from the tower.`, choices:[`Taxi clear of the runway in use`,`Stop`,`Return to starting point`], answer:0, why:`Flashing red on the ground means taxi clear of the runway in use. Steady red is stop. Flashing white is return to starting point.`},
      {prompt:`On the ground, a flashing white light from the tower.`, choices:[`Exercise extreme caution`,`Return to starting point on the airport`,`Cleared for takeoff`], answer:1, why:`Flashing white means return to your starting point on the airport. It has no meaning in flight.`},
      {prompt:`Your transmitter is dead. In flight, in daylight, how do you acknowledge a light signal?`, choices:[`Blink the landing light`,`Move the rudder`,`Rock the wings`], answer:2, why:`In flight by day, rock the wings (AIM 4-2-13). On the ground by day, move the ailerons or rudder. At night, blink the landing or navigation lights.`},
      {prompt:`Four yellow lines across a taxiway, two solid and two dashed. You're taxiing toward the runway, and the solid lines are nearest you.`, choices:[`Hold here until cleared onto the runway`,`You're clear of the runway`,`Taxi across without stopping`], answer:0, why:`The solid lines are on the side where you must hold. At a towered airport you don't cross toward the runway without a clearance.`},
      {prompt:`White arrows down the centerline lead to a white bar across the runway.`, choices:[`Closed pavement, no use at all`,`Usable for takeoff and rollout, not for touchdown`,`Usable for taxi only`], answer:1, why:`That's a displaced threshold. The arrow area is available for takeoff in either direction and for landing rollout from the opposite direction, but you don't touch down before the bar.`},
      {prompt:`Yellow chevrons painted on pavement lined up with the runway.`, choices:[`Unusable for landing, takeoff, or taxi`,`A displaced threshold`,`An area for runup`], answer:0, why:`Chevrons mark pavement that isn't usable for landing, takeoff, or taxi, like a blast pad.`},
      {prompt:`A PAPI shows three white lights and one red.`, choices:[`Slightly low`,`On glidepath`,`Slightly high`], answer:2, why:`Two white and two red is on glidepath, about 3 degrees. Three white is slightly high, about 3.2. Four white is high.`},
      {prompt:`Runway edge lights turn yellow near the far end of an instrument runway.`, choices:[`Caution zone for the last 2,000 feet or half the runway`,`A taxiway intersection ahead`,`The runway is closed beyond them`], answer:0, why:`On instrument runways yellow replaces white on the last 2,000 feet or half the length, whichever is less, as a caution zone.`},
      {prompt:`A red sign with white letters reading 17L-35R, next to the hold lines.`, choices:[`You're on taxiway 17L`,`Runway holding position, with 17L's end to the left`,`Direction to runway 17L`], answer:1, why:`Red with white is a mandatory instruction sign. The numbers are arranged to match the runway: the 17L threshold is to your left and 35R is to your right.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`While in flight, a pilot receives a steady red light signal from the tower. This means`, opts:[`give way to other aircraft and keep circling`,`the airport is unsafe, so do not land there`,`return for landing and expect a steady green`], a:0, why:`91.125: steady red in flight means give way to other aircraft and continue circling. Flashing red in flight is airport unsafe. Flashing green is return for landing.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`An airplane's transmitter has failed. In flight, during daylight, the pilot acknowledges a tower light signal by`, opts:[`blinking the landing light`,`rocking the wings`,`moving the rudder`], a:1, why:`AIM 4-2-13: in flight during daylight, rock the wings. At night, blink the landing or navigation lights. Moving the ailerons or rudder is the daytime acknowledgment on the ground.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`The recommended way to enter the traffic pattern at a non-towered airport is`, opts:[`descending on a long straight in to final`,`descending into downwind from above pattern altitude`,`at a 45 degree angle to downwind, at midfield`], a:2, why:`AIM 4-3-3 and AFH chapter 8: enter level at pattern altitude, at 45 degrees to the downwind leg, abeam the midpoint of the runway. Descending entries are a collision hazard.`, task:`III.B`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Two airplanes of the same category are converging at about the same altitude, and neither is head on. Which has the right of way?`, opts:[`The faster airplane`,`The airplane on the other's right`,`The airplane on the other's left`], a:1, why:`91.113(d): when aircraft of the same category are converging at approximately the same altitude, the aircraft to the other's right has the right of way.`, task:`III.B`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`When landing behind a large jet on the same runway, the pilot should stay`, opts:[`at or above the jet's final approach path and land beyond its touchdown point`,`below the jet's final approach path and land before its touchdown point`,`at or above the jet's path and land before its touchdown point`], a:0, why:`AIM 7-4-6: stay at or above the larger aircraft's final approach path, note its touchdown point, and land beyond it. Vortices sink and stay near the touchdown zone.`, task:`III.B`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Wingtip vortices are strongest when the generating aircraft is`, opts:[`light, dirty, and fast`,`heavy, dirty, and fast`,`heavy, clean, and slow`], a:2, why:`AIM 7-4-3: vortex strength increases with weight and decreases with speed, and a clean configuration produces the strongest vortices.`, task:`III.B`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`The portion of a runway behind a displaced threshold may be used for`, opts:[`taxiing and runup, but never for takeoff or landing`,`takeoffs either way and rollouts from the opposite end`,`landings in either direction, but never for takeoff`], a:1, why:`AIM 2-3-3: the area behind a displaced threshold is available for takeoff in either direction and landing from the opposite direction. You can't touch down on it landing toward the threshold bar.`, task:`II`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`The wind is 040° at 20 knots and the runway is 01. The crosswind component is about`, opts:[`17 knots`,`20 knots`,`10 knots`], a:2, why:`The angle is 40 minus 10 = 30 degrees. Crosswind = 20 × sin 30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 knots. The headwind is 20 × cos 30° = about 17 knots.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Using the wing low method in a crosswind landing, the airplane should first touch down on`, opts:[`the downwind main wheel`,`the upwind main wheel`,`both main wheels at once, wings level`], a:1, why:`AFH chapter 9: with the wing low method the crosswind correction is held through the roundout, and the upwind main wheel touches first. Leveling the wings lets the airplane drift.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`When a go-around is begun, the pilot's first concern is`, opts:[`applying full power`,`retracting the flaps`,`reporting to the tower`], a:0, why:`AFH: power, attitude, configuration. Full or maximum allowable power first, smoothly. Flaps come up in increments after the descent is stopped. Talk after you're climbing.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`During a forward slip to a landing with a crosswind from the left, the pilot should lower`, opts:[`the right wing and hold left rudder`,`the left wing and hold right rudder`,`either wing, whichever is comfortable`], a:1, why:`AFH chapter 9: in a forward slip with a crosswind, lower the upwind wing so the airplane is banked into the wind. Wind from the left means left wing down, with right rudder to keep the track.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, weather. The wind direction in a written METAR is referenced to`, opts:[`magnetic north, like the runway numbers`,`the runway in use`,`true north`], a:2, why:`METAR and TAF winds are true. ATIS and ASOS or AWOS voice broadcasts, and tower winds, are magnetic. Convert before comparing a METAR wind with a runway number.`, task:`I.C`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, systems. During a go-around in an airplane with a carburetor, carburetor heat should be`, opts:[`turned off to get maximum power`,`left on to protect against icing`,`turned on if it was off`], a:0, why:`AFH chapter 9: carburetor heat is turned off to obtain maximum power during a go-around. Heated air is less dense and costs power.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, emergencies. The wing flaps fail to extend and the airplane is high on final. The best way to steepen the descent without gaining airspeed is`, opts:[`lowering the nose to dive down to the glidepath`,`a forward slip`,`reducing power and raising the nose`], a:1, why:`AFH chapter 9: a slip dissipates altitude without increasing airspeed and is useful when flaps are inoperative. Lowering the nose builds speed. Raising the nose with power off slows the airplane toward a stall.`, task:`IX`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Spaced review, performance. A pilot goes around just above the runway and pulls up hard right away. The airplane may not climb because`, opts:[`induced drag rises as it leaves ground effect`,`ground effect adds parasite drag near the runway`,`the airspeed indicator over-reads in ground effect`], a:0, why:`AFH chapter 9: the cushion near the runway is reduced induced drag in ground effect, borrowed performance that's repaid as the airplane climbs out of it. Climbing too soon can leave it unable to climb or hold altitude at full power.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She shut Biscuit down at the tie down at 8:40. Mixture to cutoff, mags off, master off, and the quiet came in all at once, the way it always did, like a door closing on a loud room. Nine landings that morning. Thirty one hours at the end of September. Thirty eight now.`,
    `Abby didn't get out. She pulled Jordan's logbook out of the flight bag between the seats, uncapped her green pen, and started writing on her knee. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Sit a minute. I'm doing your book."</span>`,
    `So Jordan sat. She reached into her flight bag for the green notebook. She'd been reading it in order again since August, a few entries at a time, and she'd stopped last week at a page with a coffee ring on it. She opened it there. For once she didn't have to go looking.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from October, nine years ago">October 14. KAPA, pattern, 1.1, solo. 290 at 16 gusting 24, landing 28 because I am a genius. Landings 1 and 2: fine. Landing 3: the worst landing ever done by a human person in a Cessna. Gust in the flare, ballooned, I held it there like an idiot because there was runway left, and she quit flying at like 10 feet. Bounce one. Bounce two (bigger, how). Bounce three I added power and sort of made it a landing out of spite. [cartoon: Biscuit drawn as a jackrabbit with wings, hopping down a runway marked 28, three arcs, each one taller than the one before, little stars at every touchdown. In the window, a stick man with his hair straight up. Over at the edge of the page, a tiny stick man behind a fence holding up two fingers. Arrow to him: WALT.] Walt was at the fence. He holds up fingers for your landings. That was a 2. He says he has never given a 10 and never will, it's "a philosophical position." Marisol checked the nosegear after and said if I'd hit the nosewheel first she'd have killed me, and I believe her. Lesson, in big letters so I see it: after the first bounce, GO AROUND. Go-arounds are free. Bounces are $$$ and also Walt.</prop>`,
    `She read it again, slower. Halfway down she laughed out loud, one bark, the kind that gets you looked at in a waiting room. Abby glanced over, decided not to ask, and went back to her pen.`,
    `Two fingers. Jordan looked out at the end of the hangar row. Walt was still there, collar up, hands back in his pockets, same as both Saturdays. She'd seen that hand go up after landings and filed it as a wave, the kind you give somebody you've decided not to like. She ran the mornings back like a monitor strip. ${(s.picks||{})[8]===1?`A week ago, after the balloon, both hands out and staying out.`:`A week ago, after the balloon, hands in pockets.`} And just now, the ninth landing, when she'd been too busy to count. Seven. She was almost sure it was seven.`,
    (s.flags.forcedlanding||G.forcedlanding)
      ?`She thought about the balloon on 28, and the half second she'd spent trying to save a landing that was already gone. Danny had spent the same half second, nine years ago, on the same runway, and then two more bounces. The difference was that Abby had been sitting next to her. Next time, maybe nobody would be.`
      :((s.picks||{})[8]===1
        ?`She thought about the balloon on 28, and the go-around, and the half second when there had still been runway left and she'd let it go anyway. Nine years ago, on the same runway, Danny had held it. She hadn't. She didn't feel smart about it, exactly. She felt like she'd been handed something, and had taken it.`
        :`She thought about the balloon on 28, and the power she'd added to stretch it out, and how much of the far end had been in the windshield. Nine years ago, on the same runway, Danny had added power out of spite. Next time she'd go around. She wrote that down in her head, in big letters, the way he had.`),
    (G.ignoredwind
      ?`And the morning on 28. She'd spent the whole drive to the airport remembering May, eleven minutes over the card with both hands on the yoke. She thought maybe the remembering was what the card had been for all along.`
      :`And the morning they'd ended up on 28, twenty degrees off the wind instead of fifty. Danny picked 28 because he was a genius. She had the math on her kneeboard.`),
    (s.score>=27
      ?`It had been a good three weeks. She could feel it in her hands, which had stopped gripping the yoke somewhere around the second Tuesday and started holding it.`
      :`It hadn't been a clean three weeks, and she knew which laps she'd want back. But she could feel it in her hands anyway. Somewhere around the second Tuesday they'd stopped gripping the yoke and started holding it.`),
    `She closed the notebook and put it back in her bag.`,
    `Abby capped the pen. Then she did something she'd never done in six months: she opened her door and climbed out first, ducking under the wing, and came around the nose to Jordan's side. She pulled the door open and held the logbook out, open to the page, so Jordan could read it.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, Saturday October 10">KAPA local, N4791E, C172N. Dual received 1.5. Day landings 9. Remarks: Traffic patterns, crosswind T/O and landings, go-arounds, forward slips, no flap landing, lost comm light gun signals, wake turbulence avoidance, runway markings. Ready for pre-solo knowledge test. <i>Abby Strand, CFI</i> (green ink, the S taking up half the box)</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Pre-solo written is on the table in the briefing room. Pass it. Then next time I get out at the runup and you go around without me."</span> She held up three fingers, the way Walt did at the fence.`,
    `Jordan opened her mouth.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Deadpan, brisk, hiding warmth.">"Three. Full stop. Don't make it weird."</span>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch06.mp3`, len:`about 55 minutes`},
  next:`First Solo`
});
